      \field{annotation}{This perception study of /s/ in Danish finds that although fronting is linked to the expected meanings of femininity and gayness when situated in a male voice, these associations do not hold in the context of a prosodic style associated with immigrants, showing the typically-associated meanings of /s/ to be hegemonic and ascribed to to white middle class contexts. I use this paper as an example of research which reveals the typical meaning structure of /s/ to be white.}
